{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788213149,"nonce":"VmuPHjVLDkiXUND+Wz5C8i4OPLs=","title":"move-to accepts `queue:true`, ignores it, replaces the running job, and reports success","message":"Wave 3 — hands. Found during 4087644's acceptance run, orchestrator, BORGES,\n2026-08-31. Same FAMILY as 4087644 (a job verb reporting success for an order\nthat did not happen) but a different mechanism, so it is filed separately.\n\nMEASURED ON THE BENCH\n\nDrafted pawn running a Goto to [120,120], then:\n\n    move-to {pawns:[38946], to:[90,120], queue:true}   -\u003e accepted 1\n    pawn {sections:[\"state\"]}                          -\u003e job_queue.total 0   (paused)\n    advance {ticks:150}                                -\u003e pawn at [95,107]\n\nFrom [98,98], x FELL and z rose — the pawn is walking to [90,120], the queued\ndestination. The running Goto was discarded, nothing was enqueued, and the verb\nreported `accepted: 1` with no `rejected` row and no indication that queueing did\nnot occur.\n\nTHE CAUSE, located in our code and not in vanilla\n\n`Source/AutoRimmer/PawnOrderVerbs.cs` — `move-to` issues its job with\n\n    FloatMenuOptionProvider_DraftedMove.PawnGotoAction(to, p, dest);\n\nwhich does the whole job itself. It never reaches\n`Pawn_JobTracker.TryTakeOrderedJob`, so there is no `requestQueueing` parameter\nto pass — **and the verb never reads a `queue` argument at all.** The registry\ndoes not reject unknown keys, so `queue:true` is accepted, dropped, and the call\nreturns success.\n\nVanilla is not at fault and this is worth stating, because the 4087644 work\nestablished that `TryTakeOrderedJob` DOES queue properly: with\n`requestQueueing:true` and a busy pawn, the first branch is skipped\n(`num2 \u0026\u0026 (!isDownEvent || flag2)` is false once `isDownEvent` is true and the\npawn is not idle) and control reaches `if (isDownEvent) { jobQueue.EnqueueLast(job, tag); return true; }`.\nConfirmed on the same bench in the same session: `wear {queue:true}` behind a\nrunning wear grew `job_queue.total` from 0 to 1 with the queued row carrying its\nown `job_def` and `job_id`. **So the argument is honoured by one verb and\nsilently dropped by its sibling.**\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS\n\n4.1's playbook and checklist are being authored against this verb surface now.\n\"Queue these three moves\" returns three successes and performs one move. A\nchecklist step that stages a multi-leg movement — the obvious way to route a\ncolonist around a threat, or to stage a caravan — silently does the last leg\nonly, and nothing in the result or the journal says so.\n\nFIX SHAPE, and there is a real choice here\n\nEither:\n1. Route `move-to` through `TryTakeOrderedJob(job, JobTag.DraftedMove, requestQueueing)`\n   so the flag is honoured — but note `PawnGotoAction` does more than build a job\n   (it is the provider's own action and carries the destination-picking that\n   `RCellFinder.BestOrderedGotoDestNear` feeds), so this needs care to avoid\n   re-implementing the widget; or\n2. Refuse `queue:true` on `move-to` with a gate naming the reason, exactly as the\n   4087644 gates do.\n\nOption 2 is honest and cheap; option 1 is the capability the caller wanted.\n**Either is acceptable; silently accepting the argument is not.**\n\nA THIRD THING TO CHECK WHILE IN THERE: whether any other verb accepts `queue`\nand drops it. The registry's typed-args layer rejects wrong TYPES but not\nunknown KEYS, so this failure mode is invisible by construction and there may be\nmore of it.\n\nACCEPTANCE\n\n- `move-to {queue:true}` behind a running job either enqueues (and `job_queue.total`\n  grows, with the queued row naming its own `job_def`) or is REFUSED with a gate\n  and a reason. Demonstrated live, with the running job still running afterwards\n  in the refuse case.\n- The reproduction above is re-run and the pawn walks to the FIRST destination\n  before the second.\n- Every other job verb that accepts a `queue` argument is audited and listed in\n  the closing comment with which behaviour it has.\n- Zero red errors.","files":null}]}